1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,564
Two identical fizzy vitamin tablets are dropped into glasses of water at 20 degrees Celsius.

2
00:00:05,564 --> 00:00:11,189
One is whole; the other has been crushed into powder. Predict: which will stop fizzing first?

3
00:00:11,189 --> 00:00:12,206
Pause and decide.

4
00:00:13,266 --> 00:00:17,514
Watch. The crushed tablet fizzes much more strongly than the whole one.

5
00:00:18,573 --> 00:00:23,659
The crushed tablet stops fizzing after 24 seconds. The whole tablet takes 62 seconds.

6
00:00:24,719 --> 00:00:26,035
Explain what you saw.

7
00:00:26,035 --> 00:00:32,377
Crushing the tablet gives it a much larger surface area, so many more particles are exposed to the water.

8
00:00:32,377 --> 00:00:36,386
There are more collisions every second, so the reaction is faster.

9
00:00:36,386 --> 00:00:41,532
Both tablets make the same amount of gas in the end, because they have the same mass.

10
00:00:41,532 --> 00:00:43,865
Pause and explain it in your own words.

11
00:00:44,925 --> 00:00:49,352
Raise the temperature, and particles gain kinetic energy and move faster.

12
00:00:49,352 --> 00:00:53,900
They collide more often and harder, so there are more effective collisions.

13
00:00:53,900 --> 00:00:57,968
An effervescent tablet fizzes much faster in hot water than in cold.

14
00:00:59,028 --> 00:01:04,174
Increase the concentration, and there are more reactant particles in the same volume,

15
00:01:04,174 --> 00:01:10,157
like a crowded room where people bump into each other more. More collisions means a faster reaction.

16
00:01:11,217 --> 00:01:17,619
Increase the surface area by breaking a lump into small pieces, and more particles are exposed to collide.

17
00:01:17,619 --> 00:01:23,961
Powdered calcium carbonate reacts faster than marble chips, though both make the same total amount of gas.

18
00:01:25,021 --> 00:01:29,867
Line A is steeper at the start and levels off sooner, so its reaction is faster,

19
00:01:29,867 --> 00:01:31,602
as with hot water or powder.

20
00:01:31,602 --> 00:01:37,226
Both lines end at the same height, because the same reactants give the same amount of product.

21
00:01:38,286 --> 00:01:43,252
When you plot results, look for any anomalous result that doesn't fit the pattern.

22
00:01:43,252 --> 00:01:47,261
It might be a measuring error, so repeat that measurement to check.

23
00:01:48,321 --> 00:01:54,065
The class repeats the experiment with whole tablets, changing only the temperature of the water.

24
00:01:55,124 --> 00:01:58,236
At 10 degrees Celsius the fizzing lasts 95 seconds.

25
00:01:58,236 --> 00:02:02,484
At 20 degrees it lasts 62, at 40 degrees 32, and at 60 degrees only 18.

26
00:02:03,543 --> 00:02:08,330
Pause and decide: what is the pattern, and how does collision theory explain it?

27
00:02:09,390 --> 00:02:14,954
Now it is your turn to shine! Pause the video after each question and try to answer it first.

28
00:02:16,014 --> 00:02:20,681
Question 1: Why is mass conserved in a chemical reaction? Pause the video now.

29
00:02:21,741 --> 00:02:25,331
Answer: Atoms are only rearranged, not created or destroyed.

30
00:02:26,390 --> 00:02:29,921
Question 2: What is activation energy? Pause the video now.

31
00:02:30,980 --> 00:02:34,451
Answer: The minimum energy needed for a reaction to start.

32
00:02:35,510 --> 00:02:40,716
Question 3: Give three factors that increase the rate of reaction. Pause the video now.

33
00:02:41,776 --> 00:02:45,964
Answer: Higher temperature, higher concentration, larger surface area.

34
00:02:47,024 --> 00:02:51,391
Question 4: Why does a reaction slow down over time? Pause the video now.

35
00:02:52,451 --> 00:02:56,101
Answer: Reactants are used up, so there are fewer collisions.
